  "summary": "The intent is sound — a clean whole-package deletion should not be refused — and the success path is well tested, but the implementation has two real defects. goBuildWholeModule ignores its worktree parameter and runs `go build ./...` in the process CWD instead of setting cmd.Dir; this is masked in production only because gt done always runs from the worktree (cwd == worktree), and it means the integration test that uses the real function verifies the host internal/cmd module rather than the temp repo, so it passes for the wrong reason. The comment asserting the empty-resolved-packages case is always a whole-package deletion is also false: go list fails for other shapes (syntax errors, build-tag-excluded files, stray non-package files), so a change whose only .go file is excluded by build constraints now proceeds to a 'verified' stamp even though the file is never compiled — a widening of the gt-h9kf refusal beyond the intended deletion case.",
